# zsh: add-zsh-hook with a correct fpath, applied to an already-defined function

Q: does add-zsh-hook break a function that is already defined?

fpath includes the unpacked function directory, so autoload succeeds.

--- session ---
fpath=(<WORKSPACE>/experiments/toolchain/root/usr/share/zsh/functions/Misc $fpath); autoload -Uz add-zsh-hook; _li_t() { echo AAA-BODY-RAN; }; add-zsh-hook precmd _li_t; echo '--- call ---'; _li_t; echo RC=$?; echo '--- when resolved ---'; whence -w _li_t
--- call ---
AAA-BODY-RAN
RC=0
--- when resolved ---
_li_t: function
%                                                                              
AAA-BODY-RAN
HOST% 

CONCLUSION: with fpath correct, add-zsh-hook runs and the function body still
executes (AAA-BODY-RAN appears). An earlier version of this experiment claimed
that add-zsh-hook replaced the definition; that was wrong -- the real failure
was simply `function definition file not found` because zsh here is an unpacked
copy whose fpath does not include its own function directory.

The adapter appends to precmd_functions/preexec_functions directly anyway,
because that works whether or not add-zsh-hook can be autoloaded, and it lets
the adapter de-duplicate by hand so re-sourcing stays idempotent.
